            <!-- Controller 1: LinuxCNC QTPlasmaC -->
            <div class="doc-section-divider" style="margin-top: 2.5rem;">
              <div class="doc-section-kicker">Controller Setup • LinuxCNC 2.9 / 2.10</div>
              <h3 class="doc-section-heading">1. LinuxCNC QTPlasmaC Configuration</h3>
            </div>
            <p>
              LinuxCNC's native <code class="mono">QTPlasmaC</code> module handles initial height sensing and torch voltage regulation through real-time HAL external offsets. RotaryCAM produces native <code class="mono">.ngc</code> programs tuned for QTPlasmaC tables:
            </p>
            <ul class="doc-bullet-list">
              <li>
                <strong>Self-Contained Cut Chart Magic Comments (<code class="mono">o=0</code>):</strong> RotaryCAM writes a self-contained magic comment at the top of your program:
                <pre><code class="language-gcode">(o=0, na=45A Mild Steel 3mm, ph=3.8, pd=0.25, ch=1.5, fr=2800, kw=1.5, th=1, ca=45, cv=115, pe=0.5, gp=75, cm=1)</code></pre>
                When QTPlasmaC loads this file, its internal G-code filter automatically populates pierce height (<code class="mono">ph</code>), pierce delay (<code class="mono">pd</code>), cut height (<code class="mono">ch</code>), cut feedrate (<code class="mono">fr</code>), arc voltage (<code class="mono">cv</code>), and gas pressure (<code class="mono">gp</code>) directly into the operator interface. You never have to manually re-type cutting numbers at the machine console.
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>Central Material Table Mode (<code class="mono">M190</code>):</strong> If your shop prefers managing consumables inside QTPlasmaC's built-in material library, enable the <em>Use Controller Material Table</em> toggle in Machine Settings. RotaryCAM will emit <code class="mono">M190 P&lt;materialNumber&gt;</code> followed by <code class="mono">M66 P3 L3 Q1</code> to sync with your database.
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>Active-High Inhibit THC Lock Polarity (<code class="mono">M62 P2</code> to Lock / <code class="mono">M63 P2</code> to Unlock):</strong>
                In LinuxCNC QTPlasmaC, digital output pin 2 (<code class="mono">motion.digital-out-02</code>) connects to <code class="mono">plasmac.thc-disable</code>. This is an <strong>active-high disable</strong> pin: the exact opposite of traditional relays! Turning pin 2 ON freezes the torch, while turning it OFF allows THC height regulation. RotaryCAM automatically handles this inverted polarity:
                <ul class="doc-bullet-list" style="margin-top: 0.35rem;">
                  <li><code class="mono">M62 P2</code>: Synchronously <strong>locks/disables THC</strong> during corner deceleration, lead-ins, 60% small bolt holes, and before torch-off.</li>
                  <li><code class="mono">M63 P2</code>: Synchronously <strong>unlocks/enables THC</strong> when the torch reaches steady cutting speed on straight runs.</li>
                </ul>
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>Small-Hole True Hole Velocity Scaling (<code class="mono">M67 E3 Q60</code>):</strong> On bolt holes <code class="mono">&le; 25.4 mm</code> (<code class="mono">1"</code>), RotaryCAM emits <code class="mono">M67 E3 Q60</code> to drop feedrate to 60% and lock the THC, restoring full speed with <code class="mono">M67 E3 Q0</code> when entering the scrap lead-out.
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>Corner Collision Prevention on Square/Rectangular Tube (<code class="mono">#&lt;keep-z-motion&gt; = 1</code>):</strong>
                By default, QTPlasmaC's G-code loader <em>strips out all Z coordinates</em> so <code class="mono">plasmac</code> can own torch height. On 4-axis square or rectangular tubing, stripping Z coordinates would cause your torch to slam directly into rotating tube corners! RotaryCAM detects when you are cutting non-round tubing or open structural profiles and automatically injects <code class="mono">#&lt;keep-z-motion&gt; = 1</code> in the header, forcing QTPlasmaC to preserve programmed Z corner climbing moves.
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>Multi-Tool Spindle Codes:</strong> RotaryCAM outputs native QTPlasmaC multi-tool codes: <code class="mono">M3 $0 S1</code> for plasma cutting, <code class="mono">M3 $1 S1</code> for pneumatic air-scribe marking (with X/Y offsets managed in HAL), and <code class="mono">M3 $2 S1</code> followed by a micro-move for stationary center-point dimples.
              </li>
            </ul>

            <!-- Controller 2: Langmuir FireControl -->
            <div class="doc-section-divider" style="margin-top: 2.5rem;">
              <div class="doc-section-kicker">Controller Setup • CrossFire, CrossFire PRO &amp; CrossFire XR (v1.6+ to v24.1.6)</div>
              <h3 class="doc-section-heading">2. Langmuir Systems FireControl Setup</h3>
            </div>
            <p>
              Langmuir Systems tables (CrossFire, PRO, and XR) run on FireControl GRBL motion control. RotaryCAM outputs hardened <code class="mono">.tap</code> programs built for FireControl <code class="mono">v1.6+</code> through <code class="mono">v24.1.6</code> so your table cuts without parser errors, false voltage faults, or GRBL serial hiccups:
            </p>
            <ul class="doc-bullet-list">
              <li>
                <strong>Parser Sentinels &amp; Strict GRBL Serial Protection:</strong> Line 1 starts with <code class="mono">(v1.6-sc)</code> and the footer after <code class="mono">M30</code> emits <code class="mono">(PS&lt;feed&gt;)</code> (unscaled surface speed, e.g. <code class="mono">(PS110)</code> or <code class="mono">(PS2800)</code>) for FireControl's speed slider. Every line allows at most one <code class="mono">(...)</code> comment (nested parens become <code class="mono">[...]</code>; semicolons <code class="mono">;</code>, slashes <code class="mono">/</code>, <code class="mono">%</code>, and <code class="mono">#</code> are stripped), filters <code class="mono">&lt; 0.025 mm</code> (<code class="mono">&lt; 0.001"</code>) micro-moves while preserving vertical plunges and refreshing modal feedrates after every IHS/plunge, and caps CrossFire PRO/XR speeds at <code class="mono">300 IPM</code> XY and <code class="mono">150 IPM</code> Z.
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>Separated IHS Backlash &amp; Thin-Sheet Springback:</strong> FireControl's two-stage IHS probe (<code class="mono">G38.2</code> + <code class="mono">G38.4</code>) keeps mechanical <code class="mono">0.020"</code> (<code class="mono">0.508 mm</code>) <strong>IHS Backlash</strong> separate from <strong>Thin-Sheet Springback</strong>. Springback automatically drops to <code class="mono">0</code> on rigid pipe, structural tubing, and heavy plate (<code class="mono">&ge; 3/16&Prime;</code> / <code class="mono">4.7625 mm</code>) so <code class="mono">SMARTV</code> (SMART Voltage) never samples an inflated cut height:
                <pre><code class="language-gcode">H0
G0 X25.000 Y10.000
G92 Z0.0
G38.2 Z-127.0 F2540.0   (Fast ohmic/switch touch probe down)
G38.4 Z12.7 F508.0      (Slow retract until switch un-trips)
G92 Z0.0
G0 Z0.508               (0.020 in IHS backlash + sheet springback if thin)
G92 Z0.0
G0 Z3.800               (Retract to Pierce Height)
M3                      (Torch Fire)
G4 P0.550               (Min 0.55s dwell on 3D ramps + Arc Transfer / Loop 1 extra)
G1 X27.500 Y10.000 Z1.500 F2800.0  (3D ramp or plunge with H0 held)
H1                      (THC Active only after reaching Cut Height)</code></pre>
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>3D Ramp Minimum Ignition Dwell (<code class="mono">0.55 s</code>) &amp; <code class="mono">H0</code> Ramp Protection:</strong> CrossFire Gen2 tables have no hardware Arc OK wire and look for cutting voltage shortly after <code class="mono">M3</code>. On 3D ramp entries, RotaryCAM enforces a mandatory <code class="mono">0.55 s</code> minimum <code class="mono">G4 P</code> dwell after <code class="mono">M3</code> to stop "Torch Moving Before Cutting Voltage Sensed" faults, holds <code class="mono">H0</code> across the entire <code class="mono">G1 X Y Z</code> ramp, and emits <code class="mono">H1</code> only after the torch reaches cut height.
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>Timing &amp; Plasma Inverter Settle Controls:</strong> Dial in <strong>Arc Transfer Latency</strong>, <strong>Loop 1 Extra Pierce Delay</strong> (<code class="mono">firstPierceExtraDelay</code> for cold first pierces), and <strong>Post-Cut Settle Dwell</strong> (<code class="mono">G4 P</code> after <code class="mono">M5</code> so inverters with post-flow lockouts like Bestarc BTC800XP finish post-flow cleanly before the next touch-off).
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>Round-Tube <code class="mono">Y-WRAP</code> / <code class="mono">X-WRAP</code> &amp; Fixed-Calibration Chuck (<code class="mono">L_rev</code>):</strong> FireControl is a 3-axis (<code class="mono">X, Y, Z</code>) controller with no native 4th rotary axis (<code class="mono">A</code>) or <code class="mono">G93</code> mode. RotaryCAM unwraps round pipe into linear <code class="mono">G94</code> travel and prints a dynamic <code class="mono">Y-WRAP</code> or <code class="mono">X-WRAP</code> header banner. On dual-Y CrossFire PRO/XR tables where shops swap the single <strong>X-axis motor</strong> onto the rotary, select <strong>Tube Along Y</strong> for automatic <code class="mono">X-Wrap</code> mapping. For geared rotary chucks, enter your fixed calibration <strong>Travel / Rev (<code class="mono">L_rev</code>)</strong> to get exact cylindrical DRO <code class="mono">G94</code> feed scaling across any pipe diameter without touching FireControl's steps/mm, while <code class="mono">(PS&lt;feed&gt;)</code> stays at your true unscaled surface cut speed.
                <div class="doc-callout warning" style="margin-top: 0.75rem;">
                  <div class="doc-callout-title">⚠ Round Tubing Only on FireControl Motor-Swap Rotary</div>
                  <p>
                    Because a motor-swap setup replaces one lateral gantry axis with pipe rotation, the torch cannot translate sideways across a flat wall while rotating. Round pipe saddles, miters, and holes cut cleanly, while square/rectangular HSS, angle iron, and C-channel require 4 independent axes (<code class="mono">X, Y, Z, A</code>).
                  </p>
                </div>
              </li>
            </ul>

            <!-- Controller 3: Mach4 + Warp9 ESS / TMC3in1 -->
            <div class="doc-section-divider" style="margin-top: 2.5rem;">
              <div class="doc-section-kicker">Controller Setup • ArtSoft Mach4 &amp; Warp9 Ethernet SmoothStepper</div>
              <h3 class="doc-section-heading">3. ArtSoft Mach4 + Warp9 ESS / WarpRunner Setup</h3>
            </div>
            <p>
              Mach4 tables powered by the Warp9 Ethernet SmoothStepper (ESS) and TMC3in1 or WarpRunner breakout boards provide ultra-fast hardware torch height control and real-time synchronized I/O. RotaryCAM provides two dedicated built-in machine profiles:
            </p>
            <ul class="doc-bullet-list">
              <li>
                <strong>Profile A: ArtSoft Mach4 (Warp9 TMC3in1 / WarpRunner):</strong>
                Outputs native <code class="mono">m2020 (...)</code> configuration registers to communicate directly with Warp9's Lua plugin:
                <pre><code class="language-gcode">m2020 (W9_HC_LOAD_DEFAULT_CONFIG_VALUES=1)
m2020 (AD1_DELAY_ENABLED=1)
m2020 (AD1_DELAY_VALUE=0.8)
m2020 (AD2_M62_M63_ENABLED=1)
m2020 (AD3_VELOCITY_ENABLED=1)
m2020 (AD3_VELOCITY_PERCENT=85)
m2020 (TARGET_TIP_VOLTS=115.0)
G04 P0.100</code></pre>
                <em>Shop Tip:</em> RotaryCAM always places a mandatory <code class="mono">G04 P0.1</code> dwell right after <code class="mono">m2020</code> register groups so Mach4's Lua macro queue has time to write the hardware registers before the ESS motion planner starts moving.
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>Profile B: ArtSoft Mach4 (Warp9 ESS):</strong> For tables using a Proma 150, PriceCNC, or external standalone THC, RotaryCAM emits clean <code class="mono">G31</code> probing with switch-offset compensation and standard <code class="mono">M3/M5</code> or synced <code class="mono">M62/M63 P2</code> pins without any <code class="mono">m2020</code> macros.
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>Synchronous Torch &amp; Anti-Dive 2 (<code class="mono">AD2</code>) Control:</strong> Traditional <code class="mono">M3/M5</code> commands in Mach4 pause the motion planner queue while Lua executes, leaving ugly dwell marks. With Warp9 synchronized I/O enabled, RotaryCAM emits motion-synchronized <code class="mono">M62 P3</code> (torch ON) and <code class="mono">M63 P3</code> (torch OFF) on TMC3in1/WarpRunner (or <code class="mono">M62/M63 P2</code> on ESS), alongside <code class="mono">M63 P2</code> (lock THC) and <code class="mono">M62 P2</code> (unlock THC) under Anti-Dive Mode 2 for zero-hesitation cuts.
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>Rotary Feedrates &amp; Dwells:</strong> Full 4-axis <code class="mono">G93</code> inverse-time mode on all cutting moves. Dwell commands (<code class="mono">G04 P&lt;seconds&gt;</code>) are emitted in decimal seconds matching Mach4's default configuration.
              </li>
            </ul>

            <!-- Controller 4: MASSO G3 -->
            <div class="doc-section-divider" style="margin-top: 2.5rem;">
              <div class="doc-section-kicker">Controller Setup • MASSO G3 &amp; MASSO Touch Plasma</div>
              <h3 class="doc-section-heading">4. MASSO G3 + DTHC Configuration</h3>
            </div>
            <p>
              MASSO G3 is a standalone hardware controller that runs canned plasma routines instead of PC macros. RotaryCAM outputs hardened <code class="mono">.nc</code> programs built around MASSO firmware rules:
            </p>
            <ul class="doc-bullet-list">
              <li>
                <strong>3 THC Hardware Modes (Machine Settings):</strong>
                Pick the mode that matches your Z-axis and voltage hardware:
                <ul class="doc-bullet-list" style="margin-top: 0.35rem;">
                  <li><strong><code class="mono">MASSO DTHC (G200 Auto-IHS)</code>:</strong> Uses full <code class="mono">G200</code> parameter blocks so MASSO handles touch-off, pierce height, cut height, and DTHC arc-voltage tracking on flat sheet and round pipe.</li>
                  <li><strong><code class="mono">External Up/Down THC (Bare G200 + G38.2)</code>:</strong> Cancels canned auto-IHS by emitting a bare <code class="mono">G200</code> line, probes with explicit <code class="mono">G38.2</code>, and drives pierce-to-cut Z moves directly for standalone Up/Down THCs.</li>
                  <li><strong><code class="mono">Fixed Height / No THC</code>:</strong> Uses bare <code class="mono">G200</code> + <code class="mono">G38.2</code> probing with <code class="mono">M666</code> locked height (never emitting <code class="mono">M667</code>) so the torch holds your programmed Z cut height.</li>
                </ul>
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong><code class="mono">G200</code> Probing (<code class="mono">O0/O1</code>), Slag-Stop (<code class="mono">S0/S1</code>) &amp; Millisecond Dwells:</strong>
                <pre><code class="language-gcode">T112 M6
G200 F600 P3.8 D400 C1.5 V115 A0 O1 S0
M3</code></pre>
                <code class="mono">O0</code> selects a Touch Switch (floating head), while <code class="mono">O1</code> selects Plasma Ohmic probing. <code class="mono">S0</code> runs Continuous cutting, while <code class="mono">S1</code> enables Slag-Stop pauses between cuts. MASSO reads both <code class="mono">D</code> in <code class="mono">G200</code> and <code class="mono">G04 P</code> strictly in <strong>integer milliseconds</strong> (<code class="mono">D400</code> or <code class="mono">G04 P400</code> = 0.4 s).
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>Mandatory <code class="mono">M667 F&lt;thcFeed&gt;</code> &amp; <code class="mono">M666</code> Anti-Dive:</strong>
                MASSO firmware does not implement the <code class="mono">A</code> anti-dive percentage inside <code class="mono">G200</code>. To keep the torch from diving in small bolt holes, corners, and lead-outs, RotaryCAM emits explicit buffered <code class="mono">M666</code> (THC OFF) and <code class="mono">M667 F&lt;thcFeed&gt;</code> (THC ON). The <code class="mono">F</code> word on <code class="mono">M667</code> is <strong>always included</strong> (<code class="mono">75-600 mm/min</code> in <code class="mono">G21</code>, or <code class="mono">3-24 IPM</code> scaled by <code class="mono">1/25.4</code> in <code class="mono">G20</code>); omitting <code class="mono">F</code> breaks THC Z tracking on MASSO.
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>3D Ramp Lead-Ins (Option B2 vs. Opt-In Hybrid B1) &amp; Template C 4D HSS:</strong>
                <ul class="doc-bullet-list" style="margin-top: 0.35rem;">
                  <li><strong>Option B2 (Bare <code class="mono">G200</code> 3D Ramps, Default):</strong> Drops canned <code class="mono">G200</code> parameters and runs explicit <code class="mono">G38.2</code>, <code class="mono">M3</code>, <code class="mono">G04 P&lt;ms&gt;</code>, and a smooth diagonal XYZ ramp from pierce height to cut height without MASSO overriding Z.</li>
                  <li><strong>Opt-In Option B1 (Hybrid <code class="mono">C=P</code>):</strong> Sets <code class="mono">G200</code> cut height equal to pierce height (<code class="mono">C=P</code>) so MASSO runs auto-IHS and ignites at pierce height before RotaryCAM ramps down to cut height. Requires an explicit target voltage (<code class="mono">V &gt; 0</code>, never <code class="mono">V0</code> auto-sampling).</li>
                  <li><strong>Template C (Continuous 4D HSS Tubes):</strong> Square and rectangular tubes use <code class="mono">Bare G200</code> + <code class="mono">G38.2</code> + <code class="mono">G92 Z</code> surface zeroing + <code class="mono">G92.1</code> cleanup + <code class="mono">M666</code> so 4D Z corner climbs track the rotating tube face cleanly.</li>
                </ul>
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>Safe Retracts, Offset Hygiene, Rotary Unwind &amp; Bar-Advance Looping:</strong>
                Programs retract to machine top with <code class="mono">G53 G0 Z0</code>, clear temporary Z offsets with <code class="mono">G92.1</code> (never <code class="mono">G92 A0</code>, keeping rotary phase intact), and emit rotary unwinds as three separate lines (<code class="mono">G91</code> / <code class="mono">G28 A0</code> / <code class="mono">G90</code>). For chuck-fed production runs, enable <code class="mono">M30 L&lt;count&gt;</code> bar-advance looping at program end.
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>MASSO F1-Setup Checklist:</strong>
                Always keep <code class="mono">T112 M6</code> at the top of plasma files (changing tools cancels plasma mode; pneumatic air scribe uses <code class="mono">T111 M6</code>), zero out any multi-head X/Y/Z tool offsets on <code class="mono">T112</code> in F1 Setup, and avoid <code class="mono">v5.100b BETA Phase 1</code> firmware.
              </li>
            </ul>

            <!-- Controller 5: ArtSoft Mach3 -->
            <div class="doc-section-divider" style="margin-top: 2.5rem;">
              <div class="doc-section-kicker">Controller Setup • ArtSoft Mach3 R3.043.062 Plasma &amp; 4-Axis Rotary</div>
              <h3 class="doc-section-heading">5. ArtSoft Mach3 Setup</h3>
            </div>
            <p>
              Tens of thousands of plasma tables still run reliably on ArtSoft Mach3. RotaryCAM's hardened Mach3 post (<code class="mono">.tap</code>) handles THC wiring differences, prevents <code class="mono">G52</code>/<code class="mono">G92</code> probe conflicts, and prints a mode-aware <code class="mono">(CONFIG: ...)</code> checklist right in the file header:
            </p>
            <ul class="doc-bullet-list">
              <li>
                <strong>4-Topology Mach3 THC Hardware Selector (Machine Settings):</strong>
                Standard <code class="mono">M101/M102</code> VBScript macros force Mach3 to stop dead before toggling pins, burning blowout holes. Pick the topology that matches your control cabinet:
                <ul class="doc-bullet-list" style="margin-top: 0.35rem;">
                  <li><strong><code class="mono">M11Px / M10Px</code> Motion-Synced (Pins 1-6 + Polarity Invert):</strong> Fires <code class="mono">M11 P#</code> (ON) and <code class="mono">M10 P#</code> (OFF) at the exact microsecond the next axis move begins. RotaryCAM emits <code class="mono">G93</code> before <code class="mono">M11</code> and never leaves a stranded <code class="mono">M11</code> right before <code class="mono">M5</code>.</li>
                  <li><strong>CandCNC DTHC DCC (<code class="mono">S900/S10/S20</code> + Optional <code class="mono">S3xx</code> Voltage + Polarity Invert):</strong> Commands CandCNC digital THC lock, unlock, and optional target arc voltage using <code class="mono">S</code> words without motion pauses.</li>
                  <li><strong>ESS Proma 150 / TMC3in1:</strong> Emits stationary <code class="mono">M102/M101</code> before <code class="mono">M3</code> and activates Internal Anti-Dive locking on small bolt holes, 3D ramps, and 4D rotary cuts.</li>
                  <li><strong>ESS Proma Compact THC SD:</strong> Enforces a mandatory <code class="mono">G31</code> touch-off on every pierce and toggles the auxiliary bypass relay (<code class="mono">M7/M9</code>) during corner slowdowns and lead-outs.</li>
                </ul>
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>ESS <code class="mono">G31</code> vs. LPT <code class="mono">G28.1</code> Probing &amp; Bowed-Stock Depth Tip:</strong>
                On a <strong>Warp9 Ethernet SmoothStepper (ESS)</strong>, always use <strong><code class="mono">G31</code> Straight Probe</strong> instead of legacy LPT <code class="mono">G28.1</code> floating-head homing. Set your ESS probe depth to <strong><code class="mono">100-127 mm</code> (<code class="mono">4-5 in</code>)</strong> so touch-offs never air-fault on bowed sheet or warped tubing. RotaryCAM adds a <code class="mono">&ge; 0.50 mm</code> (<code class="mono">0.02 in</code>) <code class="mono">G31</code> stroke guard with surface-relative probing.
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>Mach3 <code class="mono">R3.043.062</code> Rotary &amp; General Config Checklist:</strong>
                Verify these console settings (summarized in your file's <code class="mono">(CONFIG: ...)</code> header) before pressing Cycle Start:
                <ul class="doc-bullet-list" style="margin-top: 0.35rem;">
                  <li><strong>Version &amp; License:</strong> Run <strong>Mach3 <code class="mono">R3.043.062</code></strong> with an active license file (Demo mode caps files at 500 lines and can drop rotary motion).</li>
                  <li><strong>Dwells &amp; Trajectory Blending:</strong> Leave <code class="mono">G04 Dwell in ms</code> <strong>UNCHECKED</strong> (dwells output in decimal seconds), set <strong>LookAhead</strong> to <code class="mono">200</code>, and keep <strong>CV Feedrate OFF</strong>. RotaryCAM emits clean <code class="mono">G64</code> without syntax-breaking <code class="mono">P</code> words.</li>
                  <li><strong><code class="mono">Use Radius for Feedrate</code> Rule:</strong> Keep <strong>Use Radius for Feedrate UNCHECKED</strong> during <code class="mono">G93</code> inverse-time rotary cuts. If you ever run a <code class="mono">G94</code> round-pipe cut using Mach3's radius DRO, enter <code class="mono">+0.0001</code> instead of <code class="mono">0</code> so Mach3 engages feed scaling.</li>
                </ul>
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>Clean Program Framing, <code class="mono">G94</code> Restore &amp; <code class="mono">G54</code> Scribe Offsets:</strong>
                Every <code class="mono">.tap</code> file wraps with <code class="mono">%</code> framing and a trailing <code class="mono">M30\n%\n</code> newline, restores <code class="mono">G94</code> immediately after <code class="mono">M5</code> and coaxial scribe strokes, filters sub-<code class="mono">0.002 mm</code> (<code class="mono">0.0001 in</code>) <code class="mono">G93</code> linear noise, and bakes pneumatic scribe XY offsets directly into <code class="mono">G54</code> coordinates to avoid Mach3's <code class="mono">G52</code> vs. <code class="mono">G92</code> register conflict.
              </li>
            </ul>

            <!-- Controller 6: Centroid Acorn -->
            <div class="doc-section-divider" style="margin-top: 2.5rem;">
              <div class="doc-section-kicker">Controller Setup • Centroid Acorn &amp; Acorn Six CNC12 v5.4+ Plasma</div>
              <h3 class="doc-section-heading">6. Centroid Acorn CNC12 Setup</h3>
            </div>
            <p>
              Centroid Acorn and Acorn Six running CNC12 v5.4+ Plasma utilize high-speed optical encoder feedback and dedicated canned macros for industrial reliability. RotaryCAM generates native <code class="mono">.nc</code> programs that drop straight into CNC12:
            </p>
            <ul class="doc-bullet-list">
              <li>
                <strong>Plasma Smoothing &amp; Material Profiles (<code class="mono">G64 ON</code> &amp; <code class="mono">M65</code>):</strong> Centroid CNC12 uses the syntax <code class="mono">G64 ON</code> for trajectory smoothing. In the preamble, RotaryCAM calls <code class="mono">M65</code> to load the operator's active material profile from CNC12's Profile Manager, synchronizing pierce heights and cutting voltages.
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>Controller-Managed Canned Cycles (<code class="mono">M61</code> &amp; <code class="mono">M62</code>):</strong>
                Instead of scattered probing and dwell lines, Centroid post-processors call Centroid's compiled macros:
                <ul class="doc-bullet-list" style="margin-top: 0.35rem;">
                  <li><code class="mono">M61</code> (<code class="mono">mfunc61.mac</code>): Automatically performs ohmic/floating touch-off, retracts to pierce height, fires the torch, verifies Arc OK, waits the pierce delay, plunges to cut height, and enables THC (<code class="mono">M35</code>).</li>
                  <li><code class="mono">M62</code> (<code class="mono">mfunc62.mac</code>): Automatically locks THC (<code class="mono">M36</code>), shuts off the plasma arc (<code class="mono">M5</code>), and retracts the Z-axis to Safe Height.</li>
                </ul>
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>Rectangular Tube Dynamic Corner Safety:</strong> On 4-axis square and rectangular tubing where the Z-axis must climb and descend rotating tube corners, RotaryCAM calls <code class="mono">M61</code> to touch off and pierce, then immediately calls <code class="mono">M36</code> (Disable THC) so Centroid's fiber-optic THC does not fight programmed Z corner moves, followed by <code class="mono">M62</code> at the end of the cut.
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>Integrated Marking &amp; Scribe Macros:</strong> Centroid CNC12 includes dedicated marking macros: <code class="mono">M64</code> performs an automated low-power plasma center-point dimple cycle, while <code class="mono">M39</code> (extend &amp; apply offset) and <code class="mono">M40</code> (retract) control pneumatic air scribes.
              </li>
            </ul>

            <!-- Controller 7: grblHAL -->
            <div class="doc-section-divider" style="margin-top: 2.5rem;">
              <div class="doc-section-kicker">Controller Setup • 32-Bit Microcontrollers (Teensy, ESP32, STM32, RP2040)</div>
              <h3 class="doc-section-heading">7. grblHAL (32-Bit Plasma + Rotary) Setup</h3>
            </div>
            <p>
              grblHAL brings high-performance 32-bit motion control and real-time plasma features to affordable breakout boards running Teensy 4.1, ESP32, STM32, and RP2040. RotaryCAM produces native <code class="mono">.nc</code> files tailored to grblHAL's core motion planner and official Plasma THC plugin:
            </p>
            <ul class="doc-bullet-list">
              <li>
                <strong>Ohmic &amp; Switch Touch-Off Probing (<code class="mono">G38.2</code>):</strong> Emits clean surface touch-off probing (<code class="mono">G38.2 Z-100 F&lt;rate&gt;</code>) followed by your configured switch offset and <code class="mono">G92 Z&lt;surface&gt;</code> to establish the true metal top datum before every pierce.
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>Arc Control &amp; Stationary Pierce Dwell:</strong> Standard <code class="mono">M3</code> torch fire and <code class="mono">M5</code> torch off, paired with precise stationary pierce dwells in decimal seconds (<code class="mono">G4 P&lt;sec&gt;</code>) before ramping or plunging to cut height.
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>Motion-Synchronized THC Anti-Dive Corner Lock (<code class="mono">M63 P0</code> / <code class="mono">M62 P0</code>):</strong>
                grblHAL's Plasma THC plugin uses digital output pin 0 for hardware THC lock. RotaryCAM uses motion-synchronized commands to avoid hesitation:
                <ul class="doc-bullet-list" style="margin-top: 0.35rem;">
                  <li><code class="mono">M63 P0</code>: Synchronously <strong>locks/inhibits THC</strong> when decelerating into tube corners, sharp direction reversals, and 60% small bolt holes to prevent the torch from diving into the cut.</li>
                  <li><code class="mono">M62 P0</code>: Synchronously <strong>unlocks/enables THC</strong> when the torch reaches steady cutting speed on flat runs.</li>
                </ul>
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>Rotary Feedrates &amp; Optional Y-Wrap:</strong> Full 4-axis <code class="mono">G93</code> inverse-time mode coordinates rotational speed with linear travel. For 3-axis grblHAL boards running a rotary chuck swapped onto the Y motor driver, RotaryCAM can unroll round-tube cuts into linear circumferential Y moves (<code class="mono">Y = R &middot; A_rad</code>) in standard <code class="mono">G94</code>.
              </li>
            </ul>

            <!-- Controller 8: CNCdrive UCCNC -->
            <div class="doc-section-divider" style="margin-top: 2.5rem;">
              <div class="doc-section-kicker">Controller Setup • CNCdrive UC100, UC300ETH &amp; AXBB-E</div>
              <h3 class="doc-section-heading">8. CNCdrive UCCNC Configuration</h3>
            </div>
            <p>
              CNCdrive motion controllers (UC100, UC300ETH, AXBB-E) running UCCNC Plasma deliver smooth S-curve trajectories and native hardware torch height control. RotaryCAM generates clean <code class="mono">.nc</code> programs ready to drop into UCCNC:
            </p>
            <ul class="doc-bullet-list">
              <li>
                <strong>Touch-Off Probing (<code class="mono">G31</code>):</strong> Executes straight ohmic or floating-head touch-off via <code class="mono">G31 Z-100 F&lt;rate&gt;</code>, compensating for your machine's switch offset before referencing Z zero.
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>Torch Fire &amp; Pierce Timing:</strong> Controls plasma ignition with standard <code class="mono">M3</code> and <code class="mono">M5</code>, accompanied by explicit decimal-second pierce dwells (<code class="mono">G04 P&lt;sec&gt;</code>).
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>Native THC Anti-Dive Corner Locking (<code class="mono">M206</code> / <code class="mono">M205</code>):</strong>
                UCCNC provides dedicated internal macro commands for controlling THC height regulation directly from G-code without pausing motion:
                <ul class="doc-bullet-list" style="margin-top: 0.35rem;">
                  <li><code class="mono">M206</code>: <strong>Freezes/disables THC</strong> during corner deceleration, lead-ins, and small-radius cutouts.</li>
                  <li><code class="mono">M205</code>: <strong>Resumes/enables THC</strong> height regulation once full cutting speed is achieved on straight runs.</li>
                </ul>
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>Pneumatic Air-Scribe Offsets (<code class="mono">G52</code>):</strong> For multi-tool setups equipped with a pneumatic air scribe, RotaryCAM automatically emits <code class="mono">G52 X&lt;dx&gt; Y&lt;dy&gt;</code> work coordinate shifts during scribing and clears them with <code class="mono">G52 X0 Y0</code> before firing the plasma torch.
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>Rotary Kinematics:</strong> Native <code class="mono">G93</code> inverse-time feedrate scaling maintains constant surface speed across flat faces and round tube profiles.
              </li>
            </ul>

            <!-- Controller 9: FlashCut CNC Plasma -->
            <div class="doc-section-divider" style="margin-top: 2.5rem;">
              <div class="doc-section-kicker">Controller Setup • FlashCut CNC &amp; Fabrication Head</div>
              <h3 class="doc-section-heading">9. FlashCut CNC Plasma Setup</h3>
            </div>
            <p>
              FlashCut CNC plasma systems with the Fabrication Head handle touch-off sensing, torch ignition, and optical voltage THC inside controller cycles. RotaryCAM exports compliant <code class="mono">.nc</code> / <code class="mono">.gc</code> files for your FlashCut table:
            </p>
            <ul class="doc-bullet-list">
              <li>
                <strong>One-Click <code class="mono">.xml</code> Setup Import (Machines Modal):</strong>
                Upload your FlashCut controller <code class="mono">.xml</code> setup file directly inside the <strong>Machines</strong> modal. RotaryCAM automatically extracts your <code class="mono">M101 I&lt;n&gt;</code> arc-transfer input, plasma head ID (such as <code class="mono">Hplasma1</code>), verifies <code class="mono">ZeroLiftAxisPositionAfterTouchOff</code>, and sets your rotary axis orientation (<code class="mono">Tube Along X</code> vs. <code class="mono">Tube Along Y</code>).
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>Mode 2 Explicit Dynamic-Z for 3D Ramp &amp; Square/Angle Tubes (<code class="mono">G631 &rarr; G602 &rarr; G605 &rarr; M50 &rarr; M101 &rarr; 3D Ramp / +CutHeight Z &rarr; G603 &rarr; M51 &rarr; G0 Z &rarr; G630</code>):</strong>
                For 3D Ramp Lead-Ins and HSS square, rectangular, or angle-iron tubes, RotaryCAM runs FlashCut Mode 2 Explicit Z without emitting <code class="mono">G601</code>/<code class="mono">G600</code>:
                <ul class="doc-bullet-list" style="margin-top: 0.35rem;">
                  <li><code class="mono">G631</code> enters Explicit Dynamic-Z mode and <code class="mono">G602</code> suspends THC. <code class="mono">G605</code> probes the tube surface, <code class="mono">M50</code> ignites the torch, and <code class="mono">M101 I&lt;n&gt;</code> waits for arc transfer.</li>
                  <li>The torch executes the <code class="mono">3D Ramp / +CutHeight Z</code> plunge, resumes THC on steady runs with <code class="mono">G603</code>, shuts off with <code class="mono">M51</code>, retracts with <code class="mono">G0 Z</code>, and exits with <code class="mono">G630</code>.</li>
                </ul>
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>FlashCut Dwell Syntax (<code class="mono">G04 X&lt;sec&gt;</code>):</strong> FlashCut uses the <code class="mono">X</code> word rather than <code class="mono">P</code> for dwell times. RotaryCAM outputs pierce delays as <code class="mono">G04 X&lt;sec&gt;</code> in seconds.
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>Native THC Anti-Dive Protection (<code class="mono">G602</code> / <code class="mono">G603</code>):</strong>
                Prevents torch diving during speed drops:
                <ul class="doc-bullet-list" style="margin-top: 0.35rem;">
                  <li><code class="mono">G602</code>: <strong>Suspends THC</strong> height tracking around sharp corners, tube transitions, and small bolt holes.</li>
                  <li><code class="mono">G603</code>: <strong>Resumes THC</strong> voltage tracking on straight runs.</li>
                </ul>
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>Rotary Feedrates:</strong> Full 4-axis <code class="mono">G93</code> inverse-time mode keeps plasma surface speed constant across tube corners and pipe contours.
              </li>
            </ul>

            <!-- Controller 10: EdingCNC Plasma -->
            <div class="doc-section-divider" style="margin-top: 2.5rem;">
              <div class="doc-section-kicker">Controller Setup • EdingCNC CNC720, CNC760 &amp; Legacy Hardware</div>
              <h3 class="doc-section-heading">10. EdingCNC Plasma Configuration</h3>
            </div>
            <p>
              EdingCNC hardware controllers (CNC720, CNC760) running EdingCNC Plasma provide deterministic real-time pulse generation and integrated torch control. RotaryCAM generates native <code class="mono">.nc</code> programs ready for EdingCNC:
            </p>
            <ul class="doc-bullet-list">
              <li>
                <strong>Surface Probing (<code class="mono">G38.2</code>):</strong> Emits reliable <code class="mono">G38.2 Z-100 F&lt;rate&gt;</code> touch-off probing with switch-offset compensation to locate the top of the tube or plate before firing.
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>Torch &amp; Pierce Dwells:</strong> Standard <code class="mono">M3</code> arc ignition and <code class="mono">M5</code> shut-off, with decimal-second pierce delays (<code class="mono">G4 P&lt;sec&gt;</code>) matching EdingCNC's interpreter.
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>Native THC Anti-Dive Locking (<code class="mono">M21</code> / <code class="mono">M20</code>):</strong>
                EdingCNC manages torch height regulation through native M-codes:
                <ul class="doc-bullet-list" style="margin-top: 0.35rem;">
                  <li><code class="mono">M21</code>: <strong>Inhibits/disables THC</strong> during corner deceleration, lead-ins, and small-hole cutting to keep the torch at programmed cut height.</li>
                  <li><code class="mono">M20</code>: <strong>Enables THC</strong> when full surface speed is reached on flat runs.</li>
                </ul>
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>Rotary Motion:</strong> Native <code class="mono">G93</code> inverse-time feedrate programming guarantees uniform arc energy on all 4-axis rotary cuts.
              </li>
            </ul>

            <!-- Controller 11: PlanetCNC TNG -->
            <div class="doc-section-divider" style="margin-top: 2.5rem;">
              <div class="doc-section-kicker">Controller Setup • PlanetCNC Mk3, Mk3/4 &amp; TNG Software</div>
              <h3 class="doc-section-heading">11. PlanetCNC TNG Plasma Setup</h3>
            </div>
            <p>
              PlanetCNC Mk3 and Mk3/4 controllers running PlanetCNC TNG software offer versatile multi-axis control with integrated THC support. RotaryCAM exports native <code class="mono">.nc</code> files tailored to PlanetCNC TNG:
            </p>
            <ul class="doc-bullet-list">
              <li>
                <strong>Surface Touch-Off Probing (<code class="mono">G38.2</code>):</strong> Uses <code class="mono">G38.2 Z-100 F&lt;rate&gt;</code> to detect the workpiece top surface, applying floating-head or ohmic switch compensation automatically.
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>Torch &amp; Pierce Control:</strong> Emits standard <code class="mono">M3</code> and <code class="mono">M5</code> commands, accompanied by decimal-second pierce dwells (<code class="mono">G04 P&lt;sec&gt;</code>).
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>Native THC Anti-Dive Corner Lock (<code class="mono">M54 P0</code> / <code class="mono">M54 P1</code>):</strong>
                PlanetCNC TNG controls THC tracking states via <code class="mono">M54</code> commands:
                <ul class="doc-bullet-list" style="margin-top: 0.35rem;">
                  <li><code class="mono">M54 P0</code>: <strong>Freezes THC</strong> before entering corner slowdowns, lead-ins, and small bolt holes.</li>
                  <li><code class="mono">M54 P1</code>: <strong>Unfreezes THC</strong> once the torch reaches steady cutting speed.</li>
                </ul>
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>Rotary Kinematics:</strong> Coordinated 4-axis <code class="mono">G93</code> inverse-time feedrates prevent torch lag and dross accumulation on rotating tube stock.
              </li>
            </ul>

            <!-- Controller 12: ShopSabre WinCNC -->
            <div class="doc-section-divider" style="margin-top: 2.5rem;">
              <div class="doc-section-kicker">Controller Setup • ShopSabre Plasma Tables &amp; SideKick Rotary Attachments</div>
              <h3 class="doc-section-heading">12. ShopSabre WinCNC Plasma Setup</h3>
            </div>
            <p>
              ShopSabre plasma tables with SideKick 4-axis rotary attachments run on WinCNC controller hardware. Check your WinCNC build under <code class="mono">Help &rarr; About</code>: RotaryCAM targets <code class="mono">v3.1.06d+</code> by default and includes a legacy toggle for older installs.
            </p>
            <ul class="doc-bullet-list">
              <li>
                <strong>Units (<code class="mono">G22</code> Metric vs. <code class="mono">G20</code> Imperial, Never <code class="mono">G21</code>):</strong> WinCNC interprets <code class="mono">G21</code> as <em>centimeters</em>, which scales parts 10x too large. RotaryCAM strictly outputs <code class="mono">G22</code> for millimeters and <code class="mono">G20</code> for inches.
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>SideKick Axis Letter Check (<code class="mono">WINCNC.INI</code>: <code class="mono">A</code>, <code class="mono">W</code>, or <code class="mono">B</code>):</strong> Open <code class="mono">WINCNC.INI</code> on your controller PC and confirm which axis letter drives your SideKick rotary (<code class="mono">A</code>, <code class="mono">W</code>, or <code class="mono">B</code>). Select that matching letter in Machine Settings so rotary moves, <code class="mono">G93</code> feeds, and <code class="mono">L28.1</code> wraps command the right motor.
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>Strict Bracket Comments &amp; Program Lifecycle (<code class="mono">[...]</code>, <code class="mono">M65</code>, <code class="mono">G9 S10</code>, <code class="mono">G94</code>, <code class="mono">L28.1</code>, <code class="mono">L92</code>, <code class="mono">M66</code>):</strong> Emits <code class="mono">[square bracket]</code> comments only (zero parentheses or semicolons), <code class="mono">M65</code> init, optional <code class="mono">G9 S10</code> smoothing, <code class="mono">G94</code> restored before rapids and <code class="mono">M62</code>, <code class="mono">L28.1</code> modulo 0-360 wrap on rotary moves, <code class="mono">G4 X&lt;sec&gt;</code> decimal-second dwells, and Pack-only <code class="mono">L92</code> before <code class="mono">M66</code> program end (keeping single-part <code class="mono">G92</code> restore intact).
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>Continuous THC (<code class="mono">L37.2 D#</code>) vs. Cut Parameters (<code class="mono">L38.1</code>) &amp; Legacy Fallback:</strong>
                <ul class="doc-bullet-list" style="margin-top: 0.35rem;">
                  <li><strong><code class="mono">v3.1.06d+</code> Continuous THC (<code class="mono">L37.2 D1/D0</code>):</strong> Emits <code class="mono">L37.2 D1</code> / <code class="mono">D0</code> and inline <code class="mono">D1</code> / <code class="mono">D0</code> where <code class="mono">D1</code> = Disabled (locked) and <code class="mono">D0</code> = Enabled. Older builds can switch to legacy <code class="mono">L37</code> / <code class="mono">L36</code>.</li>
                  <li><strong>Decoupled <code class="mono">L38.1</code> Cut-Parameter Injection:</strong> Toggle <code class="mono">L38.1</code> on to push rotary cut parameters (<code class="mono">THC=... M1</code> Enabled vs. <code class="mono">M0</code> Disabled) from RotaryCAM, or turn it off to run your WinCNC material table directly.</li>
                </ul>
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>Pattern 1 (<code class="mono">M61</code>) vs. Pattern 2 (<code class="mono">L38</code> Direct Ignition Bypass):</strong>
                <ul class="doc-bullet-list" style="margin-top: 0.35rem;">
                  <li><strong>Pattern 1 (<code class="mono">M61</code>):</strong> Runs WinCNC's touch-off and pierce cycle, suppresses duplicate <code class="mono">G4 X</code> dwells, and applies centerline <code class="mono">G92 Z</code> on 4D HSS tubing and ramps.</li>
                  <li><strong>Pattern 2 (<code class="mono">L38</code>):</strong> Bypasses <code class="mono">M61</code> for direct torch ignition and automatically engages on 3D ramps whenever <code class="mono">L38.1</code> is off or legacy mode is active. <code class="mono">M62</code> shuts off the arc and retracts Z cleanly.</li>
                </ul>
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>Pneumatic Scribe (<code class="mono">G54</code> XY Baking):</strong> Bakes pneumatic air-scribe XY offsets directly into <code class="mono">G54</code> coordinates with zero <code class="mono">G52</code>, <code class="mono">G53</code>, or <code class="mono">G56</code> conflicts.
              </li>
            </ul>

            <!-- Controller 13: CSLab SimCNC -->
            <div class="doc-section-divider" style="margin-top: 2.5rem;">
              <div class="doc-section-kicker">Controller Setup • CSLab CSMIO/IP-M &amp; IP-S (SimCNC 3.5 &amp; 3.6+ G93)</div>
              <h3 class="doc-section-heading">13. CSLab SimCNC (3.5 &amp; 3.6+ G93) Setup</h3>
            </div>
            <p>
              CSLab SimCNC tables run either 4D Euclidean feedrate spoofing (SimCNC 3.5) or native <code class="mono">G93</code> inverse-time (SimCNC 3.6+). RotaryCAM hardens <code class="mono">SimCNC 3.6+ G93</code> output so every cut runs cleanly without controller alarms or motion stalls:
            </p>
            <ul class="doc-bullet-list">
              <li>
                <strong>Trajectory Blending &amp; <code class="mono">G93</code> Feed Precision:</strong> Emits <code class="mono">G69</code> plus metric <code class="mono">G64 P0.3</code> / <code class="mono">P0.4</code> blending even inside <code class="mono">G20</code> inch programs (banning <code class="mono">G61</code> exact-stop) and formats <code class="mono">G93 F</code> to <code class="mono">4</code> decimals with a <code class="mono">0.0001</code> floor.
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>Micro-Move Coalescing &amp; Axial Feathering:</strong> Coalesces sub-<code class="mono">0.05 mm</code> / <code class="mono">0.02&deg;</code> micro-segments and inserts <code class="mono">0.002 mm</code> (<code class="mono">0.0026 mm</code> in <code class="mono">G20</code>) axial feathering between pure-XYZ and pure-ABC moves to prevent SimCNC planner stalls.
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>Dwell, Comment, Parameter &amp; Arc Guardrails:</strong> Suppresses sub-<code class="mono">1 ms</code> dwells (<code class="mono">G4 P&ge;1</code> ms), limits each line to 1 <code class="mono">(...)</code> comment with strict non-comment character filtering, enforces the <code class="mono">#1999</code> parameter ceiling (with <code class="mono">#4061</code> THC allowlist and <code class="mono">G4 P1</code> sync), formats <code class="mono">G20</code> arcs to <code class="mono">5</code> decimals (<code class="mono">I/J/K</code> within <code class="mono">0.005 mm</code>, split full circles, no <code class="mono">G90.1</code>/<code class="mono">G91.1</code>), and runs phase-preserving <code class="mono">G92 A</code> unwinds (no <code class="mono">G92.1</code>, explicit <code class="mono">G0</code>/<code class="mono">G1</code> reset after <code class="mono">G80</code>).
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>Motion-Sync Macros &amp; Feedrate Guide:</strong> For <code class="mono">ExtTrajSyncOut</code> wiring (<code class="mono">M62/M63 P0/P1</code>) and 3D ramp handshakes, see <a href="simcnc-motion-sync.html" style="color: var(--cyan-400);">Tutorial 07: SimCNC Motion-Sync</a> and <a href="rotary-and-sheet-feedrates.html" style="color: var(--amber-400);">Tutorial 11: G94, G93 &amp; G20/G21 Feedrates</a>.
              </li>
            </ul>

            <!-- Scribe Section: EasyScribe vs Pneumatic -->
            <div class="doc-section-divider" style="margin-top: 2.5rem;">
              <div class="doc-section-kicker">Multi-Tool Marking • Part IDs, Bend Lines &amp; Weld Witness Ticks</div>
              <h3 class="doc-section-heading">14. Scribing: Coaxial EasyScribe vs. Pneumatic Air-Scribe Setup</h3>
            </div>
            <p>
              RotaryCAM supports both drag scribing and pneumatic impact scribing across all 12 controllers in <strong>Pro Shop ($399)</strong>, letting you engrave Part IDs, chassis bend lines, and weld fit-up witness marks directly on your parts before cutting:
            </p>

            <div class="doc-intro-grid" style="margin: 1.25rem 0;">
              <div class="doc-intro-stat">
                <span class="label">Coaxial EasyScribe (Drag)</span>
                <span class="value">Installs in Plasma Torch Cup • All 12 Controllers • Auto M0 Swap Pause</span>
              </div>
              <div class="doc-intro-stat">
                <span class="label">Pneumatic Air-Scribe (Offset)</span>
                <span class="value">Independent Air Slide • Multi-Tool M-Codes • Zero Swap Pause</span>
              </div>
            </div>

            <ul class="doc-bullet-list">
              <li>
                <strong>Coaxial Drag Scribing (<code class="mono">EasyScribe</code>):</strong>
                The EasyScribe tool replaces the shield cup and nozzle directly inside your plasma torch body. It requires zero auxiliary air valves or wiring and works on <strong>every table and controller (including Langmuir FireControl)</strong>.
                When scribing with EasyScribe:
                <ul class="doc-bullet-list" style="margin-top: 0.35rem;">
                  <li>The plasma arc is kept strictly <strong>OFF</strong> (<code class="mono">M3</code> is never fired).</li>
                  <li>The machine touches off the stock surface, plunges into the spring preload depth (<code class="mono">Z_scribe</code>, typically <code class="mono">1.0–1.5 mm</code>), drags the diamond or carbide stylus across the part contour, and retracts to Safe Z.</li>
                  <li>When scribing and cutting in the same job, RotaryCAM automatically injects an <code class="mono">M0</code> program pause with a message on your screen prompting you to swap between the EasyScribe tool and your plasma cutting consumables.</li>
                </ul>
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>Pneumatic Offset Air-Scribe:</strong>
                For high-production shops with an air-powered stylus mounted on a pneumatic slide alongside the plasma torch. RotaryCAM applies the physical <code class="mono">(X, Y, Z)</code> head offset automatically and drives the scribe using your controller's native multi-tool commands:
                <ul class="doc-bullet-list" style="margin-top: 0.35rem;">
                  <li><strong>LinuxCNC QTPlasmaC:</strong> <code class="mono">M3 $1 S1</code> (air on + slide drop) / <code class="mono">M5 $1</code> (retract)</li>
                  <li><strong>MASSO G3:</strong> <code class="mono">T111 M6</code> (switch to Air Scribe) / <code class="mono">T112 M6</code> (switch back to Plasma)</li>
                  <li><strong>Centroid Acorn:</strong> <code class="mono">M39</code> (extend &amp; offset) / <code class="mono">M40</code> (retract)</li>
                  <li><strong>Mach4 &amp; UCCNC:</strong> Auxiliary output M-code pair with automatic <code class="mono">G52 X&lt;dx&gt; Y&lt;dy&gt;</code> work coordinate shift and <code class="mono">G52 X0 Y0</code> cancellation.</li>
                  <li><strong>ArtSoft Mach3:</strong> Auxiliary output M-code pair with XY scribe offsets baked directly into <code class="mono">G54</code> coordinates (avoiding <code class="mono">G52</code> vs. <code class="mono">G92</code> probe register conflicts).</li>
                  <li><strong>ShopSabre WinCNC:</strong> Software-baked <code class="mono">G54</code> XY pneumatic scribe offsets with zero <code class="mono">G52</code>, <code class="mono">G53</code>, or <code class="mono">G56</code> conflicts.</li>
                </ul>
                Because the air scribe is permanently mounted beside the torch, your machine marks all Part IDs, bend lines, and alignment ticks, then immediately switches to the plasma torch and cuts out the parts in one completely hands-off automated run.
              </li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </article>
